<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhagavad Gita</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gita</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhagavad Gita is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scripture of 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> verses forming part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> epic, probably composed in the last centuries BCE. It records a dialogue on the eve of battle between the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> on duty, devotion and liberation. It is among the most widely read and commented religious texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita.jpg" alt="Bhagavad Gita" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> title means 'God's song'. Indian tradition attributes the Gita, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> of which it is a part, to the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> legend says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> wrote it down using one of his broken tusks. Modern scholarship regards it as a composite work by several authors that draws on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the Samkhya school. It is generally dated to the second or first century BCE, though estimates run from 400 to 500 BCE to the first century CE. It stands in the Bhishma Parva, the sixth book of the Mahabharata, and is often studied on its own in eighteen chapters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text is a dialogue between the Pandava prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and his charioteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, an avatar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, just before the Kurukshetra War. Seeing relatives, friends and teachers in the opposing army, Arjuna drops his bow and doubts whether he should fight. Krishna's answers form the rest of the work. He argues that one should follow one's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> without regarding oneself as the agent of the action, and that action and renunciation are inseparable. The poem offers three disciplines, jnana (knowledge), bhakti (devotion) and karma (action), and does not exclude any of them. In the eleventh chapter Krishna shows Arjuna his universal form.</p><p>The 700 verses are mostly in the anushtubh metre, with eight syllables to each quarter-verse, and the tristubh metre appears at dramatic moments. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> exist in many Indic scripts and vary little compared with those of the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Gita is part of the Prasthanatrayi, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the Brahma Sutras, the foundational texts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a>. The oldest surviving commentary is that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a>, about 800 CE, who read it as a text of non-dualism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a>, in the eleventh and early twelfth centuries, read it as qualified monism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhvacharya">Madhvacharya</a>, about 1250, as a doctrine of the fundamental difference between the soul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and the world. Modern commentators include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-aurobindo">Sri Aurobindo</a>.</p><p>Persian translations were made under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. Charles Wilkins published the first English translation in 1785, and French, German and Russian versions followed. The text has also been debated: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> criticised its defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> duty and of the warrior's duty to fight, while others read the battlefield as an allegory of the struggle within.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhagavad Gita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The paths to release are usually described as three: the way of action performed without attachment (karma yoga), the way of knowledge (jnana yoga) and the way of loving devotion to God (bhakti yoga), all of them taught in the <b>Bhagavad Gita</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>…longest poems in the world, tells of the war between two branches of a royal family and contains the <b>Bhagavad Gita</b>, the dialogue between the warrior Arjuna and the god Krishna; the Ramayana, attributed to the sage Valmiki, tells the story of Prince Rama and his wife Sita.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Upanishads and the epics, including the <b>Bhagavad Gita</b> of the Mahabharata, were essential to these later developments.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vedic-civilisation">Vedic civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bhagavad Gita</b>, probably composed in the last centuries BCE, presents devotion to Krishna as a path to liberation beside the paths of ritual action and knowledge, and promises that women and people of low birth who take refuge in him also reach the highest goal.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>On religion he described his beliefs as rooted in Hinduism and the <b>Bhagavad Gita</b>, held that all religions contain truth and some error, and believed that truth, ahimsa and the Golden Rule lay at the core of every religion.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/gandhism">Gandhism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Before the battle Arjuna, seeing his kin and teachers in the opposing army, refuses to fight, and his charioteer Krishna reminds him of his duty as a Kshatriya; this dialogue is the <b>Bhagavad Gita</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
